[97.237, "o", "Pip | chat | local | gemma4:e2b-it-qat\r\n/exit to end; /reset clears conversation. Chat never becomes searchable source evidence.\r\nYou> what can we do?\r\nPip | chat | local | gemma4:e2b-it-qat\r\n\r\nI'm Pip, your local AI Safety study partner. I can brainstorm, draft, plan, and revise using our recent conversation. For your notes, I can retrieve original passages and cite them.\r\n\r\nUse `ask` for an independent source-grounded answer, `search` to inspect passages without generation, and `ingest` to generate wiki drafts for review. I have no web search and do not save chat as source evidence.\r\n\r\nSuggestion: explore assistance games, compare governance ideas, or draft a 30-minute study plan.\r\nYou> what can you help me with?\r\nPip | chat | local | gemma4:e2b-it-qat\r\n\r\nI'm Pip, your local AI Safety study partner. I can brainstorm, draft, plan, and revise using our recent conversation. For your notes, I can retrieve original passages and cite them.\r\n\r\nUse `ask` for an independent source-grounded answer, `search` to inspec"]
[97.237, "o", "t passages without generation, and `ingest` to generate wiki drafts for review. I have no web search and do not save chat as source evidence.\r\n\r\nSuggestion: explore assistance games, compare governance ideas, or draft a 30-minute study plan.\r\nYou> Draft a three-step plan for a 30-minute study session.\r\nPip | chat | local | gemma4:e2b-it-qat\r\n\r\nSuggestion:\r\n\r\n1. **Review Material (10 minutes):** Quickly skim your notes or textbook sections to refresh your memory on the core concepts.\r\n2. **Active Recall/Practice (15 minutes):** Test yourself on the material using flashcards, practice problems, or by trying to explain a concept out loud.\r\n3. **Summarize & Identify Gaps (5 minutes):** Write down the main takeaways and note any areas where you felt unsure or need further review.\r\n\r\nModel time: 2.612 s\r\nYou> make that shorter\r\nPip | chat | local | gemma4:e2b-it-qat\r\n\r\nSuggestion:\r\n\r\n1. **Review (10 min):** Skim notes for core concepts.\r\n2. **Practice (15 min):** Active recall or practice problems.\r\n3. **Summarize "]
[97.237, "o", "(5 min):** Write takeaways and note gaps.\r\n\r\nModel time: 1.112 s\r\nYou> For this conversation only, my project codename is Tangerine. Acknowledge it without saving it to the wiki.\r\nPip | chat | local | gemma4:e2b-it-qat\r\n\r\nGot it. For this conversation only, your project codename is Tangerine.\r\n\r\nOriginal evidence:\r\n[Pd36e317f50b9] vault/raw/alignment-lecture-notes.md:L21-L40\r\n\r\n- Core framing: AI\u2019s only goal is to promote human interests; it is uncertain what those interests are and must infer them\r\n- \u201cInterest\u201d preferred over \u201cpreference\u201d or \u201cvalues\u201d:\r\n  - \u201cValues\u201d triggered culture-war reactions\r\n  - \u201cStated preferences\u201d are easily manipulated; \u201cinterest\u201d implicitly acknowledges that preferences can be shaped against one\u2019s own good\r\n- Inference mechanism: observe behavior and internal mental states, apply Bayesian priors\r\n- Convergence: in theory, infinite data + fixed model of human decision-making \u2192 recover true preferences; in practice, unlikely to fully converge\r\n- Non-iden"]
[97.237, "o", "tifiability problem: behavior alone can\u2019t distinguish preferences from beliefs (e.g., a chess-player example: anti-rational agent looks identical from outside)\r\n  - Bayesian prior that someone is anti-rational is infinitesimally low, so practically resolvable\r\n\r\n### Hard Problems for the Assistance Game\r\n\r\n- Preferences vs. interests: what someone prefers may not be good for them\r\n- Preference plasticity and manipulation:\r\n  - Addiction changes preference structures; AI should weight \u201creflective\u201d preferences over drug-modified ones\r\n  - Broader issue: preferences are shaped by external parties (e.g., patriarchal societies shaping women\u2019s preferences)\r\n  - Risk: AI systems are now themselves engaged in preference engineering\r\n- Aggregation and social choice: what should AI do when serving multiple humans with conflicting interests?\r\n- Autonomy dimension:\r\n  - Climbing Everest vs. being helicoptered to the top: outcomes matter, but so does agency\r\n\r\n\r\n[P393649e8c594] vault/raw/alignment-lecture-notes.md"]
